  "description": "A micro-lesson that makes CTE: The Price of Repeated Impact usable.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "One concussion heals. Hundreds of small hits do not. Years later, the brain starts to fall apart from the inside.",
      "visual": "🏈"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Chronic traumatic encephalopathy develops after repeated mild head impacts, even those too small to cause a diagnosed concussion.",
      "visual": "💥"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Each impact triggers abnormal tau protein deposits. Over years, these tangles spread through the cortex, strangling neurons.",
      "visual": "🕸️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "CTE cannot be diagnosed in a living person. Confirmation requires examining brain tissue after death.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Studies of deceased NFL players found CTE in over 90 percent of donated brains, revealing a hidden epidemic in contact sports.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "CTE teaches a brutal lesson: the danger is not one big hit, but a lifetime of small ones.",
      "visual": "⚠️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What protein accumulates abnormally in CTE?",
    "options": [
      "Tau protein",
      "Amyloid beta",
      "Alpha-synuclein"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
